Abstract:
A theoretical claim is that languaging is a source of second language (L2) learning (Swain, 2006, 2010). 


Keywords: 

second language acquisition; 
teaching methods in applied linguistics; 
sociocultural language studies

Mixed methods as an avenue of inquiry has developed into a field of its own, distinguishable from quantitative and qualitative analyses. Because this approach to research reflects a pragmatic means of accruing culturally sensitive data from multiple lenses in a single study, it may offer an important opportunity for cross-cultural and cultural psychologists. Conducting mixed methods studies allows cultural researchers to simultaneously gather broader, culturally valid quantitative data and provide additional meaning through deep, qualitative understanding. This task is not simple, however, as it requires an understanding of qualitative and quantitative methods as well as the means of integrating data into a single study. Despite the potential difficulties in intentionally combining methods in a single study, mixed methods offers cross-cultural and cultural psychologists the opportunity to gather multiple types of data, draw comparisons, and better understand culturally-relevant phenomena. 


Keywords: 

cross-cultural methodologies; 
mixed methods designs; 
pragmatic approaches to cross-cultural psychology
